<p>UK Prime Minister Keir Starmer faced growing Labour leadership pressure on Sunday after reports that he was considering setting a timetable to leave office, while he publicly continued to reject calls to resign. Daily Mail journalist Dan Hodges, cited by RT and other publications, reported that an unnamed cabinet minister said Starmer understood the political reality and wanted any departure to happen on his own terms. The pressure followed Labour’s poor performance in the May 7 local and regional elections, where Nigel Farage’s Reform UK made large gains, and a week in which nearly 90 Labour MPs called on Starmer to resign, according to France 24. France 24 reported that Starmer was also under scrutiny after policy U-turns and criticism of his appointment of a friend of Jeffrey Epstein as U.S. ambassador. Former Health Secretary Wes Streeting said he would stand in any Labour leadership contest, and Greater Manchester Mayor Andy Burnham sought a route back to Parliament through the Makerfield by-election. Streeting also broke with Starmer’s Brexit stance by saying Britain should pursue a new relationship with the European Union and could one day rejoin it.</p><img src='https://kagiproxy.com/img/NmIS6Sh3w_jd_5rM9RxaInnqkMX6HzUMt1Oc-W0oeQelCwlOT2tBf9b9YJYp8LZSfLbvpUE1cbUnqP_RPobYNtuT1pAZuVChwEvfcrJpWqyO22KsdQAI3WiECUE' alt='Keir Starmer as ministers discussed whether he should announce a departure timetable.' /><br /><h3>Highlights:</h3><ul><li>Brexit: Streeting argued that leaving the EU had made Britain less prosperous, less powerful and less in control, while News18 said the leadership crisis had pushed Brexit back into the political spotlight.</li><li>Anti-incumbent vote: RTE said Starmer’s large Commons majority concealed a weakness because voters had turned more against Conservative chaos than toward Labour’s cautious leader, while the FT pointed to cakeism, impatience and low-quality public discourse as contributors to instability.</li></ul><blockquote>Catastrophic mistake - Wes Streeting</blockquote><h3>Perspectives:</h3><ul><li>Ed Miliband: Business Times reported that Miliband, described as one of Starmer’s longtime supporters, had urged him to set a timetable to leave office. (<a href='https://www.businesstimes.com.sg/international/how-keir-starmer-imploded-and-plunged-britain-more-chaos'>Business Times</a>)</li><li>Andy Burnham: iNews reported that ministers saw Burnham’s Makerfield prospects as central to Starmer’s fate, with some expecting Starmer to be forced out if Burnham won and others warning of mayhem if Burnham lost after Starmer had committed to leaving. (<a href='https://inews.co.uk/opinion/starmer-is-deciding-whether-to-announce-a-departure-timetable-4423397'>iNews</a>)</li><li>Karl Hansen: The former Labour adviser and Tribune Magazine political writer described Starmer as a &quot;lame duck Prime Minister&quot; during a France 24 discussion of the leadership crisis. (<a href='https://www.france24.com/en/tv-shows/spotlight/20260517-uk-leadership-crisis-keir-starmer-is-a-lame-duck-prime-minister-says-former-labour-adviser'>France 24</a>)</li></ul><h3>Sources:</h3><ul><li><a href='https://www.ft.com/content/0cb0f4c5-c324-4626-9b5d-cec7726264b7'>Britain gets the politics it deserves</a> - ft.com</li><li><a href='https://www.rt.com/news/640085-starmer-considering-resigning-daily-mail/'>UK’s Starmer could quit as prime minister – Daily Mail</a> - rt.com</li><li><a href='https://www.france24.com/en/tv-shows/spotlight/20260517-uk-leadership-crisis-keir-starmer-is-a-lame-duck-prime-minister-says-former-labour-adviser'>UK leadership crisis: ‘Keir Starmer is a lame duck Prime Minister’ says former Labour adviser</a> - france24.com</li><li><a href='https://novaramedia.com/2026/05/17/british-politics-is-in-meltdown-heres-why/'>British Politics Is in Meltdown. Here’s Why.</a> - novaramedia.com</li><li><a href='https://www.thehindu.com/opinion/editorial/stuttering-starmer-on-the-labour-leadership-crisis/article7099